WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:13.580
There are few constants in League of Legends esports.

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:16.040
Between Rosters shuffles.

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:18.000
Players retiring.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:19.700
And legacies ending.

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:24.720
"That signals the end, the SKT dynasty is over."

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:28.440
But there are a few things you can always count on.

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:30.740
NA will always choke.

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:33.800
The broadcast will always be delayed.

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:40.820
And Sjokz bringing fans across the world some of the biggest moments the game has ever seen.

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:42.520
From the goofy,

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:53.080
"Whoa whoa whoa whoa whoa, too much headbanging danger for our lives."

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:54.640
To the emotional,

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:59.760
"I'm really sorry to all of my fans, that I let you down."

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:03.360
To the savage.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:06.960
"We did check your solo queue records on Jinx it wasn't fantastic,

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:08.960
But it was great in this game."

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:15.480
Sjokz is one of League of Legends’ most recognizable faces.

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:22.160
But maybe more importantly, Sjokz has helped define a key element of modern esports broadcasts

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:25.800
and brought players’ personalities to esports fans.

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:29.920
This is her story.

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:40.800
Eefje “Sjokz” Depoortere was born on June 16, 1987 in Sint-Andries, Belgium.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:45.880
Now known best as the face of the EU LCS, Sjokz didn’t start her career in League

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:51.040
of Legends as a pro, in the same way that many casters and analysts do these days.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:55.440
In fact, she didn’t start as a League of Legends fan at all since her first gaming

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:57.840
love was Unreal Tournament.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:03.360
As a kid growing up in Belgium, Sjokz played plenty of video games, but found her way to

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:04.820
Unreal Tournament.

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:10.750
UT was one of the first massively popular online shooting games, with plenty of guns,

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:13.540
modes and features to keep players interested.

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:19.780
Sjokz, for her part, played Instagib Capture the Flag on clan Prepare For Your Doom, even

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:24.480
playing in several LAN tournaments and winning a Nationcup with team Belgium

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:27.240
"We were Champions of the NationCup with Team Belgium,

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:31.420
So in the Nations Cup Belgium would win and actually in the Euro Cups, and on LAN parties I played my team

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:35.200
my team would always come in second to a team called Majestic 5.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:38.860
We would be in Prepare For Your Doom, and it was kind of a rivalry."

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:43.480
But after graduating from high school and going to university to study history, she

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:47.400
had to drop playing the game competitively to focus on her studies.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:51.270
But as her time at university was closing out, she began to panic.

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:54.880
Sjokz didn’t feel like she was ready for the real world.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:57.300
She felt like she needed more time.

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:01.800
She didn’t know what she wanted to do, she didn’t know where to go, so she decided

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:02.960
to stay in school.

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:05.840
"And I was very much attracted to the history side of everything,

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:08.340
So I decided to do history for four years.

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:14.620
Over the course of those four years, I actually discovered that I liked to be a sports reporter a lot more."

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:20.280
She moved to Brussels to study Journalism, then back to Ghent to get a teaching degree.

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:25.140
With six years in school and three degrees, Sjokz entered the working world.

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:28.960
But still had no idea what she wanted to do.

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:33.300
"You're  in the system where it's all about studying, getting your grades, but you also have relative

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:37.880
freedom you know you get to kind of plan your own time, and then when that's over you go okay,

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:44.920
am I gonna be, a 9-5 person, am I gonna have kids, you know I don't know live remotely and just

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:48.600
do my job and then I'll retire and all those thoughts were just in my head and I'm like,

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:50.940
is this for me what do I want to do."

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:56.340
She worked various temp jobs, but when she wasn’t working, Sjokz was at home, playing

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:57.560
video games.

00:03:57.640 --> 00:04:03.320
And then, An old friend from her Unreal Tournament days introduced Sjokz to a new game.

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:17.800
League of Legends wasn’t new in 2011, but it was still growing, and its pro scene was

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:19.200
still being built out.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:22.900
Sjokz had never played a MOBA before, but she was hooked.

00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:28.220
The competition, the scene, it reminded her of her time playing Unreal Tournament in her

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:29.980
care-free high school years.

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:32.680
"One of my old clan mates said, "This is free you should try it.

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:36.540
it's fun," and basically I was really attracted to the fact that it was just really fun

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:42.760
It was a really good game, to play with five people, to be on TeamSpeak and you know it was a learning curve

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:48.560
I was really bad, I mean I've never been great at League of Legends, but it was just so incredibly fun."

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:54.600
By day, Sjokz waited tables, cleaned houses and answered phone calls, but by night?

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:58.480
She was back to staying up late, playing games with friends.

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:07.440
And then one fateful night, Sjokz stumbled across a stream that changed her life.

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:11.080
"Must have been in 2012 and it's in Hanover and uhm,

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:17.520
I think it was Counter Logic Gaming but it was like HotshotGG and Saintvicious and all those people so

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:24.080
really like old school and I remember watching a lot of that in my first couple of you know discovery steps

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:25.160
competitive League."

00:05:25.480 --> 00:05:30.100
This game that Sjokz had been playing for months now had a competitive scene, just like

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:33.540
Unreal did, but it was bigger in every way.

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:39.820
Tournaments were more glamorous, prizes were higher, and perhaps most notably, the superstars

00:05:39.820 --> 00:05:41.040
were already there.

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:45.500
She wasn’t the best player in the world, but she knew in that moment, that she needed

00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:47.980
to be part of the League of Legends community.

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:49.380
So she got to work.

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:54.530
Sjokz found her way to SK Gaming, who hired her to write news about tournaments and team

00:05:54.530 --> 00:05:55.530
changes.

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:59.120
It was a small job, but she made it into something more.

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:04.800
Her articles eventually got her a job as SK’s lead editor for League of Legends, and eventually

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:09.840
into the role that let her truly break into the scene: the host of Summoner’s Recap.

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:13.240
Hey guys, I'm Sjokz and this is the Summoner's Recap,

00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:16.320
your weekly League of Legends news fix in just a couple of minutes."

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:20.560
"Hey guys and welcome to the Summoner's Recap live from Austin Texas."

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:26.080
"Hey you guys, I'm Sjokz and this is the Summoner's Recap, still your weekly League of Legends newscast."

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:30.480
Summoner’s Recap was an early video show for League fans looking to get caught up on

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:31.500
the scene.

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:36.979
It wasn’t perfect, but the community rallied around it for both its content and the personality

00:06:36.980 --> 00:06:37.700
behind it.

00:06:37.700 --> 00:06:39.820
"French casting duo Chips and Noi,

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:43.220
French... Chips et Noi?"

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:46.540
Sjokz cared about League, and it showed.

00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:51.060
Which meant more people came knocking at her door to get her in their videos.

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:56.400
She was a regular guest on Whose League is it Anyway, a popular League personality talk show.

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:04.760
"Yes hello, my name is Eefje 'Sjokz' Depoortere and I'm drinking Martini Bleu which is I know some kind of

00:07:04.760 --> 00:07:08.940
Not really champagne, just a little under that but still really good.

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:12.800
And you know cause we have something to celebrate man, we all did good this year."

00:07:13.060 --> 00:07:17.540
And got a chance to start travelling to events and interviewing players at tournaments around

00:07:17.540 --> 00:07:18.160
Europe.

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:23.940
"Hey guys this is Sjokz, and I'm here with our star player Ocelote who is feeling a bit better than he was

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:27.020
yesterday but probably still not totally over what happened."

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:33.120
Those opportunities got her a gig covering US esports tournaments for the CyberSportsNetwork,

00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:35.020
going to MLG Dallas

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:40.100
"Hey guys this is Sjokz here at MLG Dallas with IWillDominate, jungler for team Dignitas."

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:41.460
Lone Star Clash

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:46.880
"Hey guys this is Sjokz here for CyberSportsNetwork, from the Lone Star Clash 2 with Aphromoo, AD Carry.

00:07:46.900 --> 00:07:47.940
For Team Fear."

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:50.640
And the IPL Finals in Las Vegas

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:55.660
"I've been here since, I arrived two days ago, and yesterday was like the training

00:07:55.660 --> 00:08:01.180
getting used to stuff day and it was really fun. We played some arcade games got to know a lot of new people so."

00:08:01.580 --> 00:08:08.580
Going back home, Sjokz’s parents and government were putting pressure on her to find traditional, steady work.

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:12.840
The whole time, she was still working temp jobs while covering League of Legends,

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:14.940
and she was running out of money.

00:08:14.940 --> 00:08:20.260
"They were right in essence because I was like in that hybrid between being a student

00:08:20.260 --> 00:08:26.860
and between being a freelancer and there wasn't really a lookout to a steady job so things were getting

00:08:26.860 --> 00:08:30.420
Things were getting quite difficult I knew a decision or I had to

00:08:30.420 --> 00:08:33.560
find a job or I had to quit and leave everything behind."

00:08:33.560 --> 00:08:37.140
With the LCS on the horizon, things looked great for players,

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:42.720
but content creators, Sjokz included, just weren’t sure what it would do to their space.

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:45.620
In December, Sjokz went to IEM Cologne

00:08:45.620 --> 00:08:50.580
when one of the producers approached her to do a quick interview with a fan for the broadcast.

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:55.740
"So at one point I was approached by one of the higher ups or people

00:08:55.740 --> 00:09:00.600
involved with the broadcast thing at ESL. And they just said, "Do you want to do this (I believe it was a fan

00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:02.640
interview or like a fan check in)"

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:07.480
And then that kinda qualified as a sort of first step or audition,

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:14.240
I wouldn't say I completely didn't realise I definitely just kind of realised it but I wasn't that savvy

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:17.580
in like business wise in what they were trying to do so I just did a good job,

00:09:17.900 --> 00:09:21.320
apparently, and that came back to me later on."

00:09:21.740 --> 00:09:25.640
ESL was gearing up to run the first season of the EU LCS,

00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:31.400
and just a few weeks after that interview, she was hired to do player interviews on the broadcast.

00:09:31.480 --> 00:09:33.400
And she’s never looked back.

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:41.640
Since joining the EU LCS broadcast, Sjokz has become one of the biggest names in League of Legends.

00:09:41.640 --> 00:09:46.900
She’s on League of Legends’ fans screens every week, whether it’s for LCS matches

00:09:47.180 --> 00:09:53.180
"Hello everyone, I'm Eefje 'Sjokz' Depoortere and to the second day of our week 2 coverage

00:09:53.180 --> 00:09:56.280
of the League of Legends European LCS Summer Split."

00:09:56.400 --> 00:09:57.480
All-stars,

00:09:57.480 --> 00:09:58.980
"I'm Eefje 'Sjokz' Depoortere

00:09:58.980 --> 00:10:05.500
on duty and it's here on this stage that we'll be crowning the winners of the 2013 League of Legends

00:10:05.500 --> 00:10:07.200
All-Star tournament later today."

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:08.780
The analyst desk,

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:10.640
"I have an amazing poker face."

00:10:12.520 --> 00:10:15.540
"it's actually really good, like I'm not even saying it."

00:10:15.820 --> 00:10:18.900
"I'm not even saying it just to say it's actually really good."

00:10:22.420 --> 00:10:23.920
Or hosting Worlds.

00:10:24.320 --> 00:10:30.000
"Welcome ladies and gentlemen to the League of Legends Season 3 World Championship.

00:10:31.340 --> 00:10:35.660
Which means she’s also been there for some of League’s biggest moments.

00:10:35.660 --> 00:10:40.820
Sjokz was one of the voices that kicked off the inaugural season of the EU LCS, marking

00:10:40.820 --> 00:10:44.900
a new era of not just League of Legends, but esports in general.

00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:50.140
That same year, she was also one of the host of Worlds, the most-watched esports event

00:10:50.140 --> 00:10:54.720
in history to that point, played in a sold-out Staples Center.

00:10:56.680 --> 00:11:01.800
"Ladies and gentlemen welcome to the Season 3 World Championship Finals!"

00:11:05.400 --> 00:11:07.100
I'm Eefje 'Sjokz' Depoortere

00:11:07.100 --> 00:11:11.360
coming to you live from the Staples Centre here Los Angeles California."

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:17.060
Being there effectively made Sjokz one of the faces of professional League of Legends.

00:11:17.180 --> 00:11:22.420
She was on every broadcast, every fan and casual watcher got to recognize her

00:11:22.420 --> 00:11:23.700
between games.

00:11:23.700 --> 00:11:28.760
"Just making one person's day, is the best feeling in the world like when we have a show

00:11:28.760 --> 00:11:33.080
and then the fans are watching they come and they're like, "Can we take a picture." or "Can we talk to you guys

00:11:33.080 --> 00:11:33.640
for a second."

00:11:33.640 --> 00:11:36.080
Of course that's the coolest thing about the job that  you see.

00:11:36.080 --> 00:11:39.380
You just talk to these people who love the exact same thing that you love,

00:11:39.700 --> 00:11:40.900
every single day.

00:11:40.900 --> 00:11:42.800
There's no better feeling in the world."

00:11:42.840 --> 00:11:47.800
It also showed off just how good she could be at adapting to bad situations,

00:11:47.800 --> 00:11:51.280
"What a game to start everything off here in Europe, I mean

00:11:51.280 --> 00:11:53.920
people said that you guys didn't look as good in the off-season

00:11:53.920 --> 00:11:56.920
but it looks like you had quite a lot of preparation.

00:11:56.920 --> 00:11:59.720
tell us about preparing for this huge first match up.

00:12:00.060 --> 00:12:02.940
"Well we prepared, we practice."

00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:05.080
"It's kind of annoying but you'll be fine."

00:12:05.100 --> 00:12:09.500
"We prep- You should have taken first interview with this noise."

00:12:09.820 --> 00:12:12.340
From there, her personality began to shine through.

00:12:12.340 --> 00:12:17.540
Sjokz has said that developing a personal connection to the players she interviewed was important,

00:12:17.540 --> 00:12:23.260
and that always comes through in the absolutely brutal slams she delivers to certain players.

00:12:23.780 --> 00:12:26.080
"I just assume that they're really good cause they've always been good."

00:12:26.080 --> 00:12:29.180
"Wasn't 50/50 for Alternate yesterday I'll tell you that."

00:12:29.360 --> 00:12:31.200
"Ohhhhhh..."

00:12:31.380 --> 00:12:33.140
"Hey this isn't fair."

00:12:33.140 --> 00:12:35.900
"Gotta be honest you guys got smashed by Gambit there."

00:12:35.900 --> 00:12:38.640
"And what did you take from that, what did you learn from that."

00:12:38.920 --> 00:12:42.000
"Smashed? Oh well?"

00:12:43.480 --> 00:12:46.860
"We have bragging rights, we won three games, they won in points."

00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:48.600
"Everyone's a winner."

00:12:48.860 --> 00:12:51.060
"Ahhh... That's something losers would say."

00:12:51.900 --> 00:12:57.420
And at Worlds 2015, Sjokz showed she can be more than just the savage, fun interviewer.

00:12:57.420 --> 00:12:59.940
She stood in front of an emotional Dyrus,

00:12:59.940 --> 00:13:04.820
offering him a literal shoulder to cry on while he gave his retirement speech.

00:13:07.260 --> 00:13:09.780
"I'm really sorry to all of my fans,

00:13:10.060 --> 00:13:11.420
that I let you down."

00:13:16.200 --> 00:13:22.220
"Dyrus, it's only normal for someone like you

00:13:22.220 --> 00:13:25.960
who tries so hard their best to feel this way in this particular moment.

00:13:25.960 --> 00:13:32.260
But all these people, really genuinely, support you. And there are so many people who do because you've built

00:13:32.260 --> 00:13:35.780
up an incredible career, every single NALCS game.

00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:41.720
Six times in the finals in the NALCS. 5 times at the World Championship,

00:13:42.160 --> 00:13:46.480
that can't be underestimated. And they've always been there for you. What do you want to say to them?"

00:13:47.080 --> 00:13:50.100
"Now it's time to open a new book.

00:13:50.620 --> 00:13:52.360
Right now my story ends here."

00:13:53.860 --> 00:13:59.280
"I think, there is nothing left to say, you have influenced the lives of so many people and you'll

00:13:59.280 --> 00:14:02.100
continue to do that thank you so much willing to do this."

00:14:02.100 --> 00:14:06.840
"And one more time for Dyrus, his incredible career, and the fact that he was willing to talk to us here."

00:14:10.700 --> 00:14:14.020
"I don't wanna take anything away from that moment and Dyrus the way it was

00:14:14.020 --> 00:14:17.760
but it was definitely also a very important career moment for me, it's when

00:14:18.040 --> 00:14:23.680
I also got a bit of recognition for being able to kind of gel in all circumstances.

00:14:23.680 --> 00:14:26.300
because of the way I handled that interview and

00:14:26.300 --> 00:14:31.560
just as a fan because people often forget that we are still fans you know, we're viewers.

00:14:31.720 --> 00:14:36.880
It was awesome to be a part of this guy you know who was an absolute huge legacy who was

00:14:36.880 --> 00:14:43.600
just opening up and at the biggest stage that existed for League of Legends, it's absolutely wonderful."

00:14:44.120 --> 00:14:48.100
League fans can always expect Sjokz on the desk,

00:14:48.100 --> 00:14:52.680
but her constant presence has helped define one of the most important things to League of Legends’

00:14:52.680 --> 00:14:56.300
continued success: the players’ personalities.

00:14:56.300 --> 00:15:01.120
"Finally they've been clapping all along so would you like to finish with a couple of words for all the TSM fans

00:15:01.120 --> 00:15:01.900
out here."

00:15:02.720 --> 00:15:03.760
"Give me this."

00:15:06.620 --> 00:15:08.780
"Not as easy as I wanted it to be."

00:15:09.560 --> 00:15:11.380
"It's not riding a bike."

00:15:11.760 --> 00:15:13.200
"Yeah I feel like a child."

00:15:16.860 --> 00:15:18.380
"That's so easy."

00:15:19.700 --> 00:15:23.360
"You remember one of the first times you were gonna be on the desk,

00:15:23.360 --> 00:15:27.720
and you were like, "I'm not sure if I should do this." And you've changed since then."

00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:28.680
"Yay!"

00:15:29.100 --> 00:15:32.820
Esports has a reputation for wooden players.

00:15:32.840 --> 00:15:39.640
The stereotype of your average esports player is that of someone cold, detached, bad at socializing.

00:15:40.060 --> 00:15:44.820
And while it’s not necessarily true of every esports athlete, it’s hard to convince people

00:15:44.829 --> 00:15:48.899
they should be watching tournaments if the players aren’t given the chance to overturn

00:15:48.900 --> 00:15:49.899
that stereotype.

00:15:50.320 --> 00:15:55.200
That’s why Sjokz interviewing players between games was so important in the early days of

00:15:55.200 --> 00:15:56.500
the EU LCS.

00:15:56.520 --> 00:16:00.320
Sure, she was doing it beforehand, but LoL’s community,

00:16:00.320 --> 00:16:03.790
was still relatively small, and her interviews weren’t raking

00:16:03.790 --> 00:16:06.100
in hundreds of thousands of views.

00:16:06.100 --> 00:16:11.520
But every one of those League fans was watching the LCS, and having Sjokz after nearly every

00:16:11.520 --> 00:16:16.839
EU game, interviewing players to humanize them did wonders for getting people attached

00:16:16.840 --> 00:16:19.940
to the real people behind the incredible plays.

00:16:20.260 --> 00:16:22.500
"You get to live through a lot with these guys,

00:16:22.500 --> 00:16:24.140
you see whenever they win or lose,

00:16:24.140 --> 00:16:27.140
you can talk to them you ask them sometimes behind the scenes how they're doing,

00:16:27.140 --> 00:16:33.360
and if they're alright. And you discover more and more step by step, but it's really a yearlong process.

